The legacy backend gives an assignment through a pointer to a char or short
the wrong value: it reuses the register holding the destination address and
overwrites only the bytes the store wrote, so '(*d = 0)' is the address with
its low byte cleared rather than 0. The store itself is correct - only the
value the expression yields is not.

That makes 'while ((*d++ = *s++) != 0)' - a strcpy, and what
test/bench/programs/strings.c is built out of - loop forever under -legacy.

The IR backend gets every case here right, so this is '.muted.legacy' rather
than '.muted': an unqualified mute would report it as a muted test that now
passes on every default-backend run.

Unmute - by deleting this file - once the legacy backend narrows an
assignment's result to the assigned type.
